    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "While the Supreme Court rulings like District of Columbia v. Heller and McDonald v. City of Chicago in 2008 recognized the individual's right to possess firearms, they also left room for certain types of gun control. Laws that impose proper regulations, such as waiting periods, background checks, and restrictions on certain types of weapons, have remained largely upheld as they don't infringe upon the core rights established by those decisions. So, while some gun control measures might face constitutional challenges, there are still valid legal arguments for enforcing regulations that aim to enhance public safety without infringing on these constitutional rights.",
